#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:1
msgid "`kernel/`"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:3
msgid "Guidelines that apply only to code under `kernel/`."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:5
msgid "Use `errno!` to construct errors (`use-errno-macro`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:7
msgid ""
"Prefer `errno!` over `Error::new` when constructing kernel errors, as it is "
"more concise:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:11
msgid "// Good\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:13
msgid "// Bad\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:18
msgid ""
"To minimize its memory footprint, `Error` cannot carry an error message. "
"However, you can document an error by including a message in `errno!`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:22
msgid "// Even better\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:23
msgid "\"the file offset is not page-aligned\""
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:26
msgid ""
"The message serves only as documentation; it is not compiled into the binary "
"and therefore incurs no runtime overhead."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:29
msgid "See also: [`error-message-format`](../naming.md#error-message-format)."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:32
msgid "Track callers when converting errors (`track-error-caller`)"
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:34
msgid ""
"The kernel `Error` type records the caller's source location when it is "
"constructed and preserves that location as the error propagates. This helps "
"developers trace a failure back to its origin, even when the error is "
"reported far from where it occurred."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:39
msgid ""
"To capture the caller's location when converting another error type into a "
"kernel `Error`, add `#[track_caller]` to every function involved in the "
"conversion, including `From::from` implementations and intermediate "
"conversion helpers. Without the attribute, the recorded location points "
"inside the conversion function instead of the caller that encountered the "
"error."
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:49
msgid "\"invalid UTF-8 string\""
msgstr ""

#: src/to-contribute/coding-guidelines/for-maintainability/path-specific/kernel.md:54
msgid ""
"See also: [Rust Reference: The `track_caller` attribute](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/reference/attributes/codegen.html#the-track_caller-attribute)."
msgstr ""
